<</if>>

You take one last glance down both sides of the street before making your move, sprinting from the bushes to the cover of a large trashcan near the street. Still clear. You move from cover to cover, quickly but cautiously. Every time you crouch down or move behind something that can’t completely cover you, you ensure your exposed lower half is covered as much as possible. <span style="color:LightGreen">Before long, you are halfway down the street, still in the clear.</span> <br>
<br>
<span style="color:Pink">A passing car causes you fall flat to the ground behind a small garden wall, the cold sidewalk on your body makes you cringe as you press up against it.</span> Nobody seems to have spotted you as you stand back up and continue creeping forward. If you can reach the street corner, you’ll have more cover and should be able to make it home, <span style="color:Pink">but the next 3 houses don’t have their trashcans out or just have a small one that wouldn’t shield you and their fences are chain-link, meaning there is hardly an ounce of cover between them!</span> <br>
<br>
<span style="color:Pink">Three houses worth of sidewalk where you’ll be utterly exposed. If a car comes driving by or someone happens to be walking about in this hour, your choices of cover are very limited. The mailboxes would do nothing and the street lights would barely cover your crotch. You could dive under a parked car, but you’d still be easily seen from the street. You glance down at your exposed body briefly before remembering you don’t really have a choice in the matter. You have to go!</span> <br>
<br>

<<linkreplace"Make a run for it!">>
    <span style="color:Pink">You take a deep breath before sprinting forward, not even caring to cover yourself as you run. If you get spotted here, there is no where to hide, so you need to go as fast as possible. You feel the early morning cool breeze hitting your skin and your bare feet plopping against the ground as you pass one house, two, nearly there…</span> <br>

